    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## The change\n\nThe spec: a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their existing household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\n**Membership level ceiling (the feature):**\n- `Owner` \u2014 added a persisted `membershipLevelCap` field/column (`membership_level_cap`) so the ceiling captured at creation is stable on later reads. Added the column to all four `schema.sql` variants (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres).\n- `AssignMembershipLevelCap` (new pipeline step) \u2014 after the household is assigned, gathers the existing (non-deleted, non-self) owners sharing the `householdId`, and if any exist stamps `membershipLevelCap = max(their capped level) + 1`. With no existing member the cap is left unset.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 wired the new step in as `membershipLevelCap`, between `household-assign` and `identity`, under the write transaction.\n- `MembershipLevels.cappedLevelFor(owner)` (new) \u2014 `min(levelFor(owner), cap)` when the cap is set, else the derived level unchanged.\n- `OwnerMapper` and `RespondWithOwnerCreated` audit \u2014 now use `cappedLevelFor` so the returned/logged `membershipLevel` is the capped value.\n\n**Unblocking the test (required to have >1 household member):**\n- `CheckUniqueIdentity` \u2014 the test creates two same-household owners (same lastName+postcode, different telephones), but this step was rejecting *any* second household member with 409 on `householdId` alone. That contradicts the documented `OwnerIdentities` semantics (\"only an exact full-key match is a duplicate; same-household owners with different telephones are both allowed\"). I aligned it to reject on the full `identityKey` (telephone + email + household) instead, so genuine duplicates are still 409'd while distinct household members are allowed.\n\n## Verification\n- `AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold` \u2014 green (owner A level 1, owner B capped at level 2 = 1+1).\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: making the test pass required relaxing `CheckUniqueIdentity` from household-based to identity-key-based rejection \u2014 a behavior change beyond the literal cap wording, but one the new test mandates and the existing `OwnerIdentities` documentation already specifies.",
